{"name":"Accolade","hex":"#7c94b2","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"accolade","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An expression of approval; praise. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A special acknowledgment; an award."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To embrace or kiss in salutation.","To confer a knighthood on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/accolade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Accolade","blocks":[{"text":"The accolade was the central act in the rite of passage ceremonies conferring knighthood in the Middle Ages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg/330px-Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accolade","wikidata":"Q547314"},{"title":"Accolade (architecture)","blocks":[{"text":"In architecture, an accolade is an embellished arch found most typically in late Gothic architecture. The term comes from French (l'accolade), referencing a \"braced\" arch. It is also known as an ogee arch (English), un arco conopial (Spanish), resaunt, arco carenato or inflesso (Italian), and kielbogen (German)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Igreja_Matriz_de_Freixo_de_Espada_%C3%A0_Cinta.jpg/330px-Igreja_Matriz_de_Freixo_de_Espada_%C3%A0_Cinta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accolade_(architecture)","wikidata":"Q30603872"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg/500px-Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg/1280px-Accolade_by_Edmund_Blair_Leighton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u